lion people in this country  who appear you know sane and you know  which side i'm talking about which  quoted  um and again i don't want to pretend  like i'm a partisan here but when you  look  uh at the comparison it's a stark  contrast and it's it's  it's cons it's hard for me uh to see  how people went the other way sometimes  but again that's just my opinion i guess  i can say that here  in this form no well i'm curious though  how do you balance that on a day-to-day  basis like when i do your show it's  pretty obvious to me that  you're a profes like you are a  professional newsman do you consider  yourself a traditional journalist still  no because it's obvious to me that you  have opinions and you're not afraid to  say them  but you are presenting news i think  there's almost an expectation now from  the audience not to give your opinion  but to be honest about where you're  coming from and i always talk about this  isn't not necessarily my opinion it's  more or less my perspective  you know giving people some insight on  where i'm coming from you know i grew up  in the deep south but my parents  are from the northeast i almost you know  i consider myself a first generation  southerner  you know even growing up in the 80s and  90s  catholic in the deep south with northern  parents you'd have friends and they'd be  somewhat skeptical of  what you know are you carpet back what's  going on here that type of thing  um but again you know it it it's  a diverse kind of background to have and  a different way of thinking at things  and that's kind of just always shaped my  perspective so but you know people  expect you to to kind of be honest  with them where you're coming from on  these things so i like to  say i do two things here i'm going to  lay out the facts for you and then i'm  going to tell you  why specifically we chose those set of  facts because  i have a limited time for my show so  there are only so many facts i can  provide in there  and there are oth